    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d418">

                <pb facs="0449" n="439" sameAs="frus:frus1944v04/pg439"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">852.75 National Telephone Company/12–544:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Spain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hayes</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Madrid</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-12-05T10:00:00+01:00">December 5,
                            1944—10 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 6—11:59 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3945. Carceller has informed Embassy verbally that he had been named by Franco to
                    resolve the telephone problem by direct negotiation with the ITT. It was pointed
                    out to him that Embassy <pb facs="0450" n="440" xml:id="pg440"/> expects Spanish
                    Government make it clear that the order of the Ministry of Labor prohibiting
                    employment of foreigners in public utilities (Embassy’s desptach 3158, September
                    30) would be repressed or interpreted as not applying to the CTNE; that there be
                    rescinded or reinterpreted the edict of the Presidency of the Government denying
                    legal validity to acts of the stockholders or directorate until such time as
                    CTNE had placed a majority of its common shares in Spain and that ITT
                    representatives in Spain should be given right to uncensored communication by
                    telephone with his principals in US. Carceller agreed to discuss first point
                    with Minister of Labor and to take under consideration second and third
                    points.</p>
                <p>In view of significance of telephone problem to political and economic relations
                    between United States and Spain Carceller requested that Embassy name an
                    observer to sit in on all conferences. He has also invited Caldwell and Ackerman
                    for luncheon on Wednesday<anchor n="67" xml:id="d418-f1"/> for an exploratory
                    talk. Latter invitation we have accepted on understanding it does not involve
                    any commitment on behalf of US Government. Our feeling is that if exploratory
                    discussion reveals a possible basis for negotiation between the parties
                    concerned Embassy forthwith should withdraw from formal participation
                    therein.</p>
                <p>Your attention is directed to Embassy’s 3944 even date.<anchor n="68"
                        xml:id="d418-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hayes</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
